\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Three views of the same factor geometry, bounded by $x+y=12$. (a) Fixed sums are concentric circles, row and column families are mirror parabolas, and the red/purple cuts are the mirror ellipses from $8x+4y=32$ and $4x+8y=32$. (b) Side view in $(X,T)$, where the multiplication table becomes a triangular lattice. (c) The same cuts as literal plane sections of $X^2+Y^2=T^2$. The figure is illustrative; the claims are proved below.}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:table_line}{{1}{2}{Three views of the same factor geometry, bounded by $x+y=12$. (a) Fixed sums are concentric circles, row and column families are mirror parabolas, and the red/purple cuts are the mirror ellipses from $8x+4y=32$ and $4x+8y=32$. (b) Side view in $(X,T)$, where the multiplication table becomes a triangular lattice. (c) The same cuts as literal plane sections of $X^2+Y^2=T^2$. The figure is illustrative; the claims are proved below}{figure.1}{}}
